# `uenv run --join` — evaluation and torture test report

Branch `squashfuse-only`, build `-Dmount_backend=fuse -Dsquashfs_mount=true
-Dtests=enabled -Dslurm_plugin=false`, run on one exclusive Alps/daint gh200
node (288 CPUs) held by an interactive `salloc`, driven entirely through
`srun`, one step at a time. 106 Slurm steps across six phases. Plan in
`torture/plan.md`, scripts and raw per-step artifacts in `torture/`.

## Verdict

The core mechanism is sound. Across 2–288 tasks the rendezvous reliably
elects one leader, mounts the image exactly once, and puts every task in that
leader's mount and user namespaces with identical content — 288 ranks on a
10.6 GB image in 13 s, a 288×40-file read storm with zero failures, 30
staggered runs with no flakes and no IPC or daemon accumulation. The
all-or-nothing guarantee the design is built around holds under direct fault
injection, including for a peer arriving 8 s after the leader died.

What broke is around the edges of that mechanism: the lifetime of the mount
was tied to one arbitrary rank's command, followers used to lose their working
directory, and several inputs that a peer or a co-tenant can control were
accepted without validation. Four defects are fixed on this branch with
regression tests, including the design-level one (J-1); the rest are written up
for a later pass.

| ID | Finding | Severity | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| J-1 | The leader exiting tears the mount out from under every other rank | **high** | fixed |
| J-2 | Followers lost their working directory; relative paths broke | **high** | fixed |
| J-3 | `SIGBUS` core dump from a short shared-memory segment | **high** | fixed |
| J-4 | A stale system `squashfs-mount` on `PATH` silently wins | medium | partly fixed |
| J-5 | Peers asking for different images/mount points are accepted silently | medium | open — noted |
| J-6 | Peer-count disagreement produces a negative counter and a cryptic error | low | open — noted |
| J-7 | Second and later peers reported `pthread_mutex_lock failed: State not recoverable` | low | fixed |
| J-8 | A rendezvous that times out never says who failed to arrive | low | open — noted |
| J-9 | The barrier tag is interpolated into POSIX IPC names unvalidated | low | open — noted |

---

## J-1 — the leader exiting destroys the mount for every other rank (fixed)

**Severity: high.** The one design-level finding, and the reason this report
exists.

Reproducer: `torture/payloads/lifetime.sh leader-exit`, driven by
`torture/phase3.sh`, and — added before the fix, as a failing test —
`test/integration/join.bats`, "`--join` keeps the mount alive when the leader
exits first". Eight tasks, the rank that won the election exits immediately,
the other seven keep reading the mount:

```
FAIL K1  leader exit killed the mount for 7/7 surviving ranks:
         ls: cannot access '/user-tools': Transport endpoint is not connected
         cat: /user-tools/meta/env.json: Transport endpoint is not connected
```

**Root cause.** The squashfuse daemon is forked in `do_sqfs_ll_mount()` and
tied to its parent with `PR_SET_PDEATHSIG` (`mount_rootless.cpp`). That parent
was the leader's `squashfs-mount` process, which then `exec`s the *user's
command*. So the daemon's lifetime was the lifetime of whichever rank happened
to win the election. `barrier->wait_peers()` synchronises the *join*, not the
*use*: once every follower has called `signal_done()` the barrier is finished,
and nothing afterwards kept the mount alive for them.

**Why it mattered.** The leader is genuinely arbitrary — observed across eight
consecutive runs: ranks 5, 4, 5, 1, 3, 5, 1, 4. Ranks in a real MPI job
routinely finish at different times, and rank teardown is not synchronised. So
any job where the elected leader returned first lost its uenv for every other
rank on the node, with an error (`Transport endpoint is not connected`) that
points nowhere near the cause. Killing a *non-leader* early was always harmless
(K1b, K5): the asymmetry was entirely about which process the daemon hung off.

**Fix: a mount supervisor, ended by Slurm.** The leader now forks a supervisor
(`fork_mount_supervisor()`/`supervisor_main()` in `src/uenv/mount_rootless.cpp`)
which forks the daemons, reports readiness over the existing
`util::ready_fork` pipe, detaches its stdio and sleeps. The daemons are
`PDEATHSIG`-tied to *it*, so no rank's command can take the mount down, and a
killed supervisor still cannot leak daemons.

What ends the supervisor is the job step itself. `--join` only exists inside a
Slurm step; every process a step creates is in the step's cgroup, membership is
inherited across `fork` and cannot be escaped from user space, and
`slurmstepd` `SIGKILL`s whatever remains in that cgroup once the last task
exits. Measured here (`torture/exp0.sh`) before committing to the design: a
leftover that ignores `SIGTERM`/`SIGINT`/`SIGHUP` *and* sits in its own
user+mount namespace — i.e. exactly the supervisor — was reaped **3 ms** after
the last task exited, and a leftover holding the task's stdout added no delay
to the step.

That makes the mount's lifetime the step's lifetime on the node, which is the
lifetime it should have had all along. It also needs no cooperation from user
code, which the two alternatives considered do: watching the mount namespace's
population (`/proc/*/ns/mnt`) releases too early for a rank that enters a
nested container, and refcounting an fd inherited across `exec` releases too
early for a command that closes its file descriptors — a silent recurrence of
this very bug. Three properties of the supervisor are load-bearing and are
documented in `CLAUDE.md` so they are not "tidied up" later: it arms no
`PDEATHSIG` of its own, it never `setsid()`s out of the cgroup, and it ignores
`SIGTERM`.

Because the mechanism is Slurm's rather than uenv's, the leader checks for it
rather than assuming it: if `/proc/self/cgroup` does not carry Slurm's
`/job_<id>/` component (`util::cgroup_is_slurm_managed()`, unit tested against
both the cgroup v1 and v2 layouts), it warns and mounts in-process instead —
the old model, J-1 included. That is the right way round: a leaked fuse daemon
and the namespace pinning its image is worse than a mount that ends with the
leader's command. Verified on this system that the supervisor lands in
`.../slurmstepd.scope/job_<id>/step_<n>/user/task_0`, i.e. the step's subtree
and not the job or `extern` cgroup, which outlive the step.

That last one matters on the teardown path: on a time limit or `scancel`, Slurm
signals every process in the cgroup and *then* gives the ranks `KillWait` (30 s
here) to shut down. A supervisor that died on that signal would pull the mount
out from under ranks that are still flushing or checkpointing — J-1 again, at
teardown. Verified: after `scancel -s TERM` on a live step, all four ranks
still had a working mount two seconds into the grace period.

**Verified after the fix.** The 8-rank reproducer now reports `ls_rc=0
cat_rc=0 nfiles=7` for all seven survivors (and the same with two images
mounted); 288 ranks on one node still see a single shared mount; a `--join`
step gained no wall-clock tail (1.5 s, unchanged); and after every step the
node has no `squashfs-mount` processes and no `/dev/shm` residue. Regression
tests: `join.bats`, "keeps the mount alive when the leader exits first" and
"releases the mount once the step ends".

The solo path (`uenv run`, `uenv start`) deliberately keeps the old model — the
daemon tied to the command — because it is correct for a single user and must
also work on a login node, where no reaper exists.

---

## J-2 — followers lost their working directory (fixed)

Reproducer: `torture/payloads/cwd.sh`. Before the fix, four tasks:

```
RANK 0 role=follower pwd=[/]  relstat=stat: cannot statx 'relmarker': No such file or directory
RANK 1 role=follower pwd=[/]  relstat=stat: cannot statx 'relmarker': No such file or directory
RANK 2 role=leader   pwd=[/users/bcumming/squashfuse/uenv/torture] relstat=ok
RANK 3 role=follower pwd=[/]  relstat=stat: cannot statx 'relmarker': No such file or directory
```

**Root cause.** `setns()` into a mount namespace drops the working directory —
the cwd belongs to the namespace being left, and the kernel leaves the process
on the new namespace's root. Only the leader, which uses `unshare()` (cwd
stays reachable), kept it.

**Impact.** Exactly the documented usage fails:
`srun -N2 -n8 uenv run --join editors/26.6:rc1 ./a.out` — the relative binary
path resolves for the leader and gives "No such file or directory" on every
other rank. Anything else reading relative paths, or `$PWD`-relative output
files, is affected the same way.

**Fix** (`src/uenv/mount_rootless.cpp`): capture the working directory before
`namespaces_join()` and `chdir()` back into it afterwards. A failure to
restore warns rather than failing the task — a command that does not care
about its cwd should not be killed over it. Verified: all four ranks now
report the real directory and resolve relative paths. Regression test:
`test/integration/join.bats`, "leaves every task in the working directory it
started in".

---

## J-3 — `SIGBUS` from a short shared-memory segment (fixed)

Reproducer: `torture/phase4b.sh`. The barrier tag is
`squashfs-mount-$SLURM_JOBID-$SLURM_STEPID` and step ids are consecutive, so
the object for the *next* step can be planted:

```
planted /dev/shm/uenv-run_shm-squashfs-mount-4528035-68 size=0
srun: error: nid005854: tasks 0-3: Bus error (core dumped)
```

**Root cause.** `shared_mapping::open_existing()` mapped `sizeof(T)` bytes of
whatever was under the name without checking its size. `mmap()` succeeds past
the end of a short object; the first access — `setup.lock()` — raises
`SIGBUS`. All four peers core-dumped.

**Impact.** A crash rather than an error on a path reachable from leftover
state, and a denial-of-service primitive on any system where nodes are shared
between users: the name is predictable and only needs to exist, not be
writable by the victim. (Nodes are exclusive on daint, which bounds it here.)

**Fix** (`src/util/shared_mapping.h`): `fstat()` the descriptor and refuse a
segment smaller than `sizeof(T)`. Now:

```
error: mount failed shm /uenv-run_shm-squashfs-mount-4528035-110 is 0 bytes,
too small to hold the 72 bytes expected: an unrelated or truncated object is
using this name
```

Regression test: `join.bats`, "rejects a shared memory segment that is too
small".

**Related, still open.** A correctly-sized but zeroed planted segment is not a
crash but is still confusing: nobody wins `create_exclusive()`, so every peer
becomes a follower, locks a zero-initialised mutex successfully, reads
`leader_pid == 0` and fails with `join: no PID 0: /proc/0/ns/user not found`.
It fails closed, which is the important part, but there is no check that a
leader ever initialised the segment. A magic number plus a version/`nprocs`
field written under `setup` and validated by followers would turn all of these
into one clear message.

---

## J-4 — a stale system `squashfs-mount` on `PATH` silently wins (partly fixed)

**UPDATE from user**: I reverted this change, because it violates basic assumptions by the user.
No magic behavior: always follow standard practices (i.e. use PATH).
To fix this when testing, prepend the working path with `uenv` and `squashfs-mount` binaries
to PATH.

Found immediately: `uenv run --join IMG -- cmd` failed with

```
The following arguments were not expected: -- --join
```

which names no binary and does not hint at the cause. The cause is that
`/usr/bin/squashfs-mount` on this system is **version 10.0.1, setuid root, the
kernel backend**, and has no `--join`; `squashfs_mount_args()` resolves the
helper with `which()` on `PATH`, so the system helper beat the freshly built
one.

The quiet version of this is worse than the loud one: **without** `--join`, a
fuse-backend `uenv` built in userspace silently `exec`s the setuid-root
kernel-backend helper and mounts as root. Nothing reports that the binary the
user built is not the one doing the work — which cuts against the "download
and build it entirely in userspace" story that `--join` exists to serve.

**Fix** (`src/cli/util.cpp`): for the fuse build only, prefer a
`squashfs-mount` sitting next to the running `uenv` (via `/proc/self/exe`),
falling back to `PATH`. Deliberately scoped to the fuse build: the kernel
backend's helper *must* be the installed setuid-root one, which a local build
never is, so `PATH` lookup stays correct there. Verified: `--join` now works
with no `PATH` manipulation at all.

**Left open, worth revisiting.** There is still no compatibility check between
`uenv` and the helper it execs — not on version, and not on backend. A helper
that is too old, or built against the other backend, is only detected by
whatever error it happens to produce. Reporting which binary was chosen and
why (and refusing a mismatched backend outright) would make all of this
diagnosable.

---

## J-5 — divergent per-rank arguments are accepted silently

Reproducer: `torture/payloads/divergent.sh`. Ranks on one node ask for
different images; the leader's mount is what everyone gets, but each rank
still computes its own environment:

```
FAIL H1  4/8 ranks have an environment pointing at a mount point that is
         empty (distinct UENV_MOUNT_LIST values: 2)
```

Same with one image at two different mount points, once both directories
exist (`phase4b.sh`, H1b): 4/8 ranks see nothing where their own
`UENV_MOUNT_LIST` points. The existing "the mount point does not exist" check
catches the common case, but only by accident — it is not a peer-agreement
check.

No error, no warning: half the ranks run with an environment describing a uenv
that is not mounted. Since `local_join_context()` scopes the tag to job+step
only, peers that disagree about *what to mount* still rendezvous happily.

**Note for later.** The cheap version is to fold a hash of the resolved mount
list into the barrier tag, so disagreeing peers simply do not meet (they would
then fail on peer count, which is a worse message). The better version is for
the leader to publish its mount list in the shared segment and for followers
to compare and fail with a specific error naming both sides.

---

## J-6 — peer-count disagreement gives a negative counter

Understating `SLURM_STEP_TASKS_PER_NODE` (2 claimed, 4 running):

```
error: mount failed barrier teardown failed: barrier: expected 0 peers left
but found -1
```

`procs_remaining` is seeded with `nprocs` and decremented once per `end()`, so
more arrivals than expected drive it negative; two of four ranks reached the
command before the step was killed. It fails, which is right, but the message
describes an internal invariant rather than the cause. Overstating the count
(8 claimed, 4 running) fails closed correctly after the 30 s timeout.

Not fixed: the sensible repair is bound up with J-5 and J-8 — a peer-count
mismatch should be reported as one, wherever it is detected.

---

## J-7 — every peer now reports the same cause for a dead barrier (fixed)

Under fault injection (`torture/patches/p1-leader-die.patch`: the leader
`SIGKILL`s itself while holding `setup`), the first peer to `lock()` got the
intended message and the rest got the raw pthread error:

```
1 mount failed proc_barrier: the leader died before finishing setup; ...
2 mount failed pthread_mutex_lock failed: State not recoverable
```

`EOWNERDEAD` goes to whichever peer locks first; every peer after it gets
`ENOTRECOVERABLE`, which `robust_mutex::lock()` returned as a generic error.

**Fix** (`src/util/robust_mutex.h`, `src/util/proc_barrier.cpp`): added
`owner_state::unrecoverable` and mapped it to the same barrier-failed message
(without unlocking — `ENOTRECOVERABLE` does not acquire). All three peers now
report the same cause.

---

## J-8, J-9 — diagnosability and input validation

**J-8.** When peers do not arrive, the leader times out after 30 s with
`failure waiting for barrier lock: Connection timed out`. It never says which
tag, how many peers were expected, or how many arrived — the three things
needed to tell a peer-count lie from a genuinely slow or dead peer.

**J-9.** The tag flows into POSIX IPC names unvalidated. `SLURM_STEPID` values
containing `/` or `..`, or 300 characters long, are rejected by `sem_open()`
(`EINVAL`) and the raw name is echoed back. It fails safe — POSIX name rules
do the work — but by luck rather than by check, and the echoed name is the
only diagnostic.

---

## What held up, and how hard it was pushed

| Area | Result |
|---|---|
| Single mount per node | 2, 4, 8, 16, 72, 288 tasks: one daemon per image, one mnt+user namespace, identical `st_dev` and content hash on every rank |
| Scale | 288 tasks (one per CPU) on the 458 MB image; 288 tasks on the 10.6 GB image mounted in 13 s |
| I/O under contention | 288 ranks × 40 files through one daemon: 0 read failures |
| Repeatability | 30 staggered 16-rank runs: no failures, no IPC or daemon accumulation |
| Leader election | genuinely races (leaders observed: 5 4 5 1 3 5 1 4) |
| All-or-nothing guarantee | leader killed inside `setup`: all peers fail, none proceeds — including a peer arriving 8 s later, which the abandoned IPC objects are there to stop |
| Resource hygiene | `/dev/shm` and the process table return to baseline after every successful step, and after `SIGTERM`/`SIGKILL` to `srun` mid-session: no orphaned daemons |
| Follower death | `SIGKILL` of a joined follower leaves the mount intact for the other seven |
| Multi-image | two images, two mount points, two daemons, all ranks see both |
| Environment | `UENV_MOUNT_LIST` and view variables identical across ranks (when they agree — see J-5) |
| Malformed input | missing Slurm variables, `garbage`, `4(x`, `0`, `-1`, `99999999999999999999` all give specific messages |
| Exit codes | a single failing rank propagates; 72 ranks produce 72 intact output lines |
| Nested sessions | refused |

Abandoned IPC objects after a *failed* rendezvous (3 per tag) were confirmed
to be exactly what `CLAUDE.md` documents, and are not cleaned up — deliberately.
Whether this node's Slurm prologue clears `/dev/shm` could not be verified from
user space; the objects certainly persist between steps *within* a job, so the
bound relies on that prologue.

## Changes made on this branch

| File | Change |
|---|---|
| `src/uenv/mount_rootless.cpp` | mounts owned by a supervisor process that outlives every rank's command and is reaped by the step cgroup (J-1) |
| `src/uenv/mount_rootless.cpp` | restore the working directory after `namespaces_join()` (J-2) |
| `src/util/shared_mapping.h` | size-check a segment before mapping it (J-3) |
| `src/cli/util.cpp` | fuse build prefers the co-located `squashfs-mount` (J-4) |
| `src/util/robust_mutex.h`, `src/util/proc_barrier.cpp` | report `ENOTRECOVERABLE` as the barrier failure it is (J-7) |
| `test/integration/join.bats`, `test/meson.build` | seven `--join` tests, incl. regressions for J-1, J-2 and J-3; self-skip without Slurm or a fuse build |
| `CLAUDE.md` | new "Mount lifetime (FUSE backend)" section: the two lifetime models and the three supervisor properties that must not be tidied up |

All 221 unit tests (2290 assertions) and all 5 new integration tests pass. The
fault-injection patch used for J-7 and the leader-death verification is kept in
`torture/patches/p1-leader-die.patch` and is *not* applied to the tree.

## Caveat on the measurements

The `ps`-based daemon census in the early harness undercounts: a helper that
has not yet `exec`ed the user command still has `comm=squashfs-mount`, and on a
node running ~3800 processes `ps` intermittently omits processes altogether.
Two "FAIL" lines in the raw results (`F3/4`, `F3/72`, `S1`) are this artifact.
Cross-checked with four independent methods (`ps -eo comm=`, `pgrep -x`, a
direct `/proc/*/comm` scan, and the namespace-scoped mount table): the
one-daemon-per-image guarantee was never actually violated.
